<mujoco model="edgeneuro_arm_hand">
  <!-- Phase 2, iteration 3 (2026-09-02): forked from mujoco_menagerie's
       unitree_g1/g1_with_hands.xml (Apache-2.0/BSD-3-Clause per that
       package's LICENSE) -- replaces the previous shadow_hand-based fork
       (see git history) after two real problems surfaced with it: (1) the
       shoulder+elbow chain welding shadow_hand's forearm on was this
       project's own hand-tuned guess (quats derived by hand, "starting-
       guess" damping/kp), not a vetted source, and it had a real bug (the
       elbow joint's rotation axis was backwards -- confirmed by rendering
       both signs, see prior git history) that went undetected for a long
       time simply because elbow_bend never read high enough in practice to
       expose it; (2) the user wanted a fully open-source, verified
       shoulder-to-hand arm rather than a custom weld.

       The FULL G1 humanoid body (pelvis/legs/waist/head/right arm) is
       included too, not just the left arm -- added after an intermediate
       version welded the bare left arm onto a small hand-made box+sphere
       "torso_reference" placeholder, which the user pointed out was its
       own unnecessary reinvention now that the real, complete body is
       already sitting right there in the same source file. Every joint
       EXCEPT the left arm's 14 (shoulder x3, elbow, wrist x3, hand x7) is
       baked into a fixed pose and its <joint> removed -- see this file's
       generation script (freeze_g1.py / assemble_final.py, not checked
       into this repo, run once to produce this XML) for how: each frozen
       body's quat has that joint's own g1_with_hands.xml "stand" keyframe
       angle composed into it via axis-angle quaternion multiplication, so
       the whole mannequin is posed as G1's own authors intended a standing
       robot to look, not qpos=0 (which -- confirmed by rendering, see the
       previous iteration's elbow/shoulder comments -- is often an odd
       folded reference pose, not a natural one). This makes the pelvis a
       plain static body (no freejoint) rigidly placed at a fixed spot in
       this scene -- there is no leg/balance physics here, deliberately:
       this project is about arm control, not bipedal locomotion, and a
       dynamic floating-base humanoid would add a whole class of falling-
       over failure modes with no relevance to the EMG+IMU arm demo this
       file exists for.

       LEFT arm, not right (2026-09-02): the real MPU6050 sensors are
       mounted on the wearer's own LEFT wrist/upper arm (per the hardware
       setup discussed throughout this project), so the simulated arm
       matches that, not a mirror of it -- the very first shadow_hand-based
       iteration used a "right hand" rig this whole time, a mismatch that
       went unnoticed since it never mattered visually until a side-by-side
       comparison came up.

       Considered apptronik_apollo before settling on G1: apollo's build is
       closer to a "prosthetic" white/black look, but its Ability Hand
       fingers are static decorative geometry with NO joints at all --
       would have silently killed the EMG grip demo.

       mesh/texture BINARY assets resolve out of the untracked
       mujoco_menagerie/ sparse checkout via the relative meshdir below
       (not duplicated into this repo) -- both shadow_hand/ (unused now,
       kept checked out from an even earlier iteration) and unitree_g1/
       live under the same mujoco_menagerie/ parent, hence meshdir pointing
       there directly and each mesh's own file="unitree_g1/assets/..." path.

       Sensor -> DOF mapping (see PRD.md Section 3, 2026-08-22 revision): IMU#1
       (upper arm) drives shoulder pitch+roll via its own gravity-referenced
       ComplementaryFilter; IMU#2 (forearm) combined with IMU#1's pitch drives
       elbow flexion (relative angle, now via the vector dot-product method --
       see phase3_control_loop_main.cpp). No magnetometer on either IMU means
       yaw is unobservable, so left_shoulder_yaw and all 3 left wrist joints
       stay fixed at 0 (documented as deliberately undriven, not an
       oversight) -- same simplification as the previous iteration, just on
       different joints. -->
  <compiler angle="radian" meshdir="../../mujoco_menagerie" autolimits="true"/>

  <!-- integrator="implicitfast" carried over from g1_with_hands.xml's own
       <option> (2026-09-02 fix): the g1_arm default class's position
       actuators use dampratio (implicit velocity damping computed from
       kp+joint inertia), which needs an implicit integrator to behave as
       intended -- explicit Euler (this scene's previous, unstated default)
       is a poor match for a stiff kp=500 system at this timestep, and was
       the real cause of the sluggish/inconsistent-looking settling seen
       while debugging the left arm's rest pose and elbow direction (see
       test_arm_kinematics.py's own git history), not a wrong axis or sign
       as first suspected. -->
  <option cone="elliptic" impratio="10" integrator="implicitfast"/>

  <default>
    <!-- Joint/position defaults copied from unitree_g1/g1_with_hands.xml's
         own "g1" default class verbatim (armature/frictionloss on joints,
         kp+dampratio+inheritrange on position actuators) rather than hand-
         picking new numbers -- these are the real robot vendor's own tuned
         values for these exact links' masses/inertias, not a guess.
         inheritrange="1" means each actuator's ctrlrange is taken directly
         from its joint's own range below. Named "g1" (matching the source
         file's own class name) since the frozen mannequin body below still
         carries childclass="g1" from that source, unedited. -->
    <default class="g1">
      <joint armature="0.01" frictionloss="0.3"/>
      <position kp="500" dampratio="1" inheritrange="1"/>
    </default>
    <default class="visual">
      <geom group="2" type="mesh" contype="0" conaffinity="0" density="0" rgba="0.75 0.75 0.78 1"/>
    </default>
    <!-- contype/conaffinity=0 (2026-09-02): this class is only used by the
         FROZEN part of the mannequin now (pelvis/legs/waist/torso/head/
         right arm -- see the left-arm-chain retarget to "collision_live"
         below). Confirmed needed by testing the widened
         SHOULDER_PITCH_RANGE/SHOULDER_ROLL_RANGE: with real collision
         enabled here, raising the live left shoulder_pitch past ~0.3rad
         (of its new 2.6704rad ceiling) made the hand/wrist physically jam
         against the frozen left hip/thigh (confirmed via data.contact, not
         guessed) -- the actuator would report its commanded ctrl but the
         arm would just stop moving there. The frozen body never moves, so
         it has nothing to gain from participating in collision at all;
         disabling it here removes that jam without touching the live arm's
         own collision behavior (still real, see collision_live below,
         needed for actually grasping the pedestal's object). -->
    <default class="collision">
      <geom group="3" type="mesh" contype="0" conaffinity="0"/>
      <!-- Foot sole contact points -- copied from g1_with_hands.xml's own
           nested "foot" default (small friction spheres at the sole, not
           full-mesh collision) since a couple of the frozen ankle/foot
           geoms still reference this class by name. Also non-colliding now
           (see this class's own contype/conaffinity above) since the feet
           never move either. -->
      <default class="foot">
        <geom type="sphere" size="0.005" priority="1" friction="0.6" condim="3"/>
      </default>
    </default>
    <!-- Real collision (default contype/conaffinity=1), used ONLY by the
         live left arm chain (retargeted from plain "collision" 2026-09-02,
         see that class's own comment for why) -- keeps the arm able to
         actually contact/grasp the pedestal's object, unlike the frozen
         body's now-inert collision geoms. -->
    <default class="collision_live">
      <geom group="3" type="mesh"/>
    </default>
  </default>

